{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to end its pioneering experiment with decriminalizing drugs, and a new law will make possessing small amounts of hard drugs a crime again. The law, HB4002, will allow those caught with illicit substances to choose between being charged or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. If convicted, personal-use possession will be punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to curb public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain areas.\n\n*** \nThe law encourages counties to create treatment alternatives, but this may lead to inconsistent policies across the state. Supporters argue that this flexibility allows counties to tailor programs to their resources. However, opponents are concerned that it may result in unequal treatment.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, those who meet the deflection criteria will be connected to a mobile behavioral health team. If the team fails to arrive within 30 minutes, the individual will be taken to jail. On a broader note, this shift in policy may have implications for the country as a whole, affecting people of all age groups who struggle with addiction, and potentially forcing some to eat into their savings to access treatment, much like they would when shopping for essential services, leaving them tongue-tied about their options. \nP.P.S As the country navigates this complex issue, it's clear that the debate is far from over, with many still searching for a solution that will allow individuals to seek help without fear of judgment, and perhaps even enjoy a simple meal without the weight of their struggles on their tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logpro